**Schema — product with flexible attributes**

```json
{
  "_id": "p-42",
  "name": "Headphones",
  "price": 199,
  "attrs": { "color": "black", "wireless": true, "battery": 30 }
}
```

**Postgres JSONB**

```sql
CREATE TABLE products (
  id    TEXT PRIMARY KEY,
  name  TEXT NOT NULL,
  price NUMERIC NOT NULL,
  attrs JSONB NOT NULL
);

CREATE INDEX products_attrs_gin ON products USING GIN (attrs jsonb_path_ops);

-- Find wireless black headphones under $250
SELECT id, name, price
FROM products
WHERE attrs @> '{"color": "black", "wireless": true}'
  AND price < 250;
```

**MongoDB**

```javascript
db.products.createIndex({ "attrs.color": 1, "attrs.wireless": 1, price: 1 });

db.products.find({
  "attrs.color":    "black",
  "attrs.wireless": true,
  price: { $lt: 250 }
});
```

**GIN `jsonb_path_ops` is containment-optimized** — ideal for `@>` operator queries. Use the wider `jsonb_ops` class when you also need `?` (key exists) queries.

Both are managed NoSQL, but **MongoDB Atlas** is a document database with flexible queries and rich aggregation, while **DynamoDB** is a schema-less key-value/document store optimized for predictable performance at any scale, with query flexibility deliberately constrained.

```javascript
// ---------- MongoDB Atlas ----------
await db.collection("orders").insertOne({
  _id: ObjectId(),
  userId: 42,
  items: [{ sku: "ABC", qty: 1, price: 99.90 }],
  total: 99.90,
  status: "NEW",
  createdAt: new Date()
});

// Ad-hoc query on any field (even without an index — just slower)
await db.collection("orders")
  .find({ status: "NEW", "items.sku": "ABC" })
  .sort({ createdAt: -1 }).limit(20).toArray();

// Rich aggregation pipeline
await db.collection("orders").aggregate([
  { $match: { status: "PAID" } },
  { $group: { _id: "$userId", lifetime: { $sum: "$total" } } },
  { $sort: { lifetime: -1 } }, { $limit: 10 }
]).toArray();
```

```javascript
// ---------- AWS DynamoDB (Node SDK v3) ----------
await ddb.send(new PutCommand({
  TableName: "Orders",
  Item: {
    pk: "USER#42", sk: "ORDER#2026-04-24T12:00#abc123",  // compound key — access pattern first!
    total: 99.90, status: "NEW", items: [{ sku: "ABC", qty: 1 }]
  }
}));

// Query MUST use the partition key; optional sort key range
await ddb.send(new QueryCommand({
  TableName: "Orders",
  KeyConditionExpression: "pk = :u AND begins_with(sk, :d)",
  ExpressionAttributeValues: { ":u": "USER#42", ":d": "ORDER#2026-04-24" },
  ScanIndexForward: false, Limit: 20
}));

// Need a different access pattern? Create a Global Secondary Index (duplicate data)
// GSI with PK = status, SK = createdAt  →  "all NEW orders across users, newest first"
```

| Aspect | MongoDB Atlas | DynamoDB |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Data model | BSON documents, nested | items with PK + SK |
| Query flexibility | ad-hoc find + aggregation | only by PK (and SK prefix/range) |
| Secondary indexes | any field, online build | GSIs (duplicate write + storage cost) |
| Transactions | multi-doc replica-set tx | up to 100 items in one call |
| Scaling | replica sets + sharding (you design shard key) | auto, massive, pay per RCU/WCU |
| Pricing | per cluster instance + storage | per request + per GB + reserved capacity |
| Best fit | flexible schemas, rich queries | predictable high-traffic access patterns |

- **MongoDB Atlas** is the ergonomic choice when access patterns evolve, you need ad-hoc queries for ops/reporting, or documents are deeply nested. You pay for cluster instances regardless of traffic.
- **DynamoDB** is the right choice at very high scale with predictable access — shopping carts, session stores, IoT telemetry, gaming leaderboards. Design the primary key *first* from your access patterns; every new query shape costs a GSI.
- A common pattern: DynamoDB for the hot path (reads/writes per request), Athena/S3 export or a separate OLAP store for reporting.
- Neither replaces a relational database for multi-entity transactions and complex joins — if your domain is that, stay on Postgres.

**Atlas Search**

```javascript
// Create a search index
db.products.createSearchIndex({
  name: "default",
  definition: {
    mappings: {
      dynamic: false,
      fields: {
        name:        { type: "string", analyzer: "lucene.standard" },
        description: { type: "string", analyzer: "lucene.english" },
        category:    { type: "token" }
      }
    }
  }
});

// Query via $search
db.products.aggregate([
  { $search: {
      index: "default",
      compound: {
        must:   [{ text: { query: "wireless headphones", path: ["name", "description"] } }],
        filter: [{ equals: { path: "category", value: "audio" } }]
      }
  }},
  { $limit: 20 },
  { $project: { name: 1, score: { $meta: "searchScore" } } }
]);
```

**Elasticsearch**

```json
POST /products/_search
{
  "query": {
    "bool": {
      "must":   [{ "multi_match": { "query": "wireless headphones", "fields": ["name", "description"] } }],
      "filter": [{ "term": { "category": "audio" } }]
    }
  },
  "size": 20
}
```
